<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;"><strong>Typical annual energy bills in Gre=
at Britain will rise by 26% in January to =C2=A32,166, </strong>the energy =
regulator has announced =E2=80=93 which was immediately described as =E2=80=
=9Can impossible high=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9Cdeeply worrying=E2=80=9D by cha=
rities.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The energy price cap will rise by 26% betw=
een 1 January and 31 March, For a typical household paying by direct debit =
for gas and electricity this means the annualised bill will rise from =C2=
=A31,723 to =C2=A32,166 (if this level was sustained for a year).</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Ofgem said the ongoing war in the Middle E=
ast is pushing up international gas prices, which are increasing more than =
electricity prices, reflecting the growing shift in electricity generation =
away from gas-fired power stations to renewables.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;"><strong>Dame Clare Moriarty</strong>, chie=
f executive of Citizens Advice, said: =E2=80=9C<em>January=E2=80=99s price =
cap hike will push energy bills to an impossible high for millions already =
struggling to afford basic essentials.</em></p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;"><em>=E2=80=9CThe [government=E2=80=99s] =
=C2=A3100 household energy payment is welcome but these sky-high prices mea=
n it will be swallowed up in less than two weeks for most households.=E2=80=
=9D</em></p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The cap affects about 20m households on st=
andard variable tariffs. About 10m homes on fixed tariffs will not be affec=
ted by this increase while their deal remains in place. The cap is updated =
every three months to reflect changes in the underlying costs of supplying =
energy.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Neil Kenward, Ofgem=E2=80=99s director gen=
eral for markets, said: =E2=80=9C<em>There are things that customers can do=
 right now which could reduce their energy bills this winter, such as consi=
dering if the predictability of a fixed tariff is right for you.=E2=80=9D</=
em></p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Households hoping for some relief in the s=
pring are likely to be disappointed, with analysts Cornwall Insight predict=
ing only a 1% fall in April, which would leave a typical bill at =C2=A32,15=
1 a year. <strong>Dr Craig Lowrey</strong>, retail markets principal at Cor=
nwall Insight, said in the long run, only a shift towards more renewable en=
ergy will make it =E2=80=9Cless likely that the next energy crisis lands on=
 people=E2=80=99s bills=E2=80=9D.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;"><strong>Asian stocks tumbled</strong>, as =
fresh US tariff threats knocked market confidence and a tech-led rally fade=
d. On Wednesday, South Korea=E2=80=99s chip-heavy Kospi index has plunged 7=
%, while Japan=E2=80=99s Nikkei lost 2.8%, China=E2=80=99s CSI 300 fell 1.6=
5% and Hong Kong=E2=80=99s Hang Seng dropped 0.7%.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Aluminium fell 4% while copper prices also=
 dropped sharply, as the fizzling AI stock rally weighed on industrial meta=
ls. Brent crude, the global oil benchmark, fell 1.56% below $90 a barrel, t=
o $89.94 a barrel.</p>
